(The vas encoder: the bytes of one x86-64 instruction.

 This file is part of Library 'vas'. It has no Library line of its own, so
 it compiles into whatever sees it: vas.vox reaches it with
   see "./encoder.vox".
 and the encoder's tests reach it the same way.

 The encoder is table-driven. Every instruction shape vas encodes is one row
 of 'the encoding rows', written in the notation of the opcode column of the
 Intel manual: the mnemonic, the patterns its operands must fit, and the steps
 that lay down its bytes. One routine reads the rows; no mnemonic has code of
 its own. When an instruction fits more than one row of its mnemonic, the
 first row it fits is the one used, so the rows are ordered the way nasm
 chooses between the forms: the accumulator and the sign-extended byte forms
 before the long ones, and the 32-bit mov before the 64-bit one.

 The operand patterns:
   r8 r16 r32 r64 xmm      a register of that kind
   r/m8 ... r/m64          a register of that kind, or memory of that size
   m16 m64                 memory of that size; m, memory of any size
   xmm/m64                 an xmm register, or memory of 64 bits
   al ax eax rax cl        that register
   1                       the number one
   imm8 imm16 imm32 imm64  a number that fits that many bits once it is
                           sign-extended to the size of the operation
   imm32u                  a number that fits 32 bits zero-extended to 64
   rel32                   the target of a jump or a call
 Memory written without a size word fits an r/m pattern only when the row's
 register fixes the size, as nasm requires; the m and xmm/m64 patterns take
 it whatever it is. A number that waits on a label is taken at the full size
 of its operation, as nasm takes it, so its size never depends on an address.

 The steps:
   two hex digits          that byte. A legacy prefix, 66, F2 or F3, is
                           written before the REX prefix; every other byte
                           after it
   REX.W                   the operation is 64 bits wide
   /r                      the ModRM byte, with the register operand in its
                           reg field; then any SIB byte and displacement
   /0 to /7                the ModRM byte, with that digit in its reg field
   +r                      the register's number added to the opcode byte
                           before it
   ib iw id iq             the immediate, in 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes
   cd                      the target, as 32 bits measured from the end of
                           the instruction
 A REX prefix is written whenever a register or the operation needs one: W
 for 64 bits, R, X and B for registers 8 to 15 in the reg field, the index
 and the base or r/m field, and a REX of its own for spl, bpl, sil and dil.

 The interface, for vas.vox and its tests:

   'prepare the encoder' with <table list> and <row mnemonics list>
       Reads the rows into the table, a list of numbers, and puts each row's
       mnemonic on the row mnemonics list. Answers the problems it found, one
       per line, or the empty text.

   'encode the instruction' with <record> and <address> and <table> and
           <row mnemonics> and <output buffer>
       Appends the instruction's bytes to the output, laid down at that
       address. Answers what is wrong with the instruction, or the empty text.

   'the size of the instruction' with <record> and <table> and
           <row mnemonics> and <scratch buffer>
       How many bytes the instruction takes, or -1 when it cannot be encoded.
       The size never depends on an address, since every jump is rel32, so
       pass one can call it before any label is placed.

 The record is the front end's instruction record: its "mnemonic" and its
 "operands", each a map with a "kind" and a "size", and by kind:
   register    "class" (r64, r32, r16, r8 or xmm) and "number", 0 to 15
   memory      "relative", "base", "index", "scale", "displacement value"
               and "displacement is constant"
   expression  "value" and "is constant"
 Pass two puts each label's address into the value it waits on before it
 calls the encoder, and leaves "is constant" false there. A memory operand
 with no register is relative to the instruction pointer when "relative" is
 true, and an absolute address when it is false; pass two marks it relative
 under default rel.

 What it costs. Every text, list and map a Vox program makes is kept until
 the program ends, and each one takes at least a page. So the encoder makes
 as few as it can: it finds a mnemonic by reading down the row mnemonics
 rather than by a key put together as text, and it keeps the facts about the
 operands in a workspace at the head of the table rather than in a list of
 their own. What it cannot avoid is reading the record, the operands list and
 one map for each operand out of it: an instruction with two operands costs
 about 24 kilobytes to encode or to measure.)

(--- Named numbers ---------------------------------------------------------)

(A library has no top-level statements, so each number with a meaning is a
 function that answers it.)

(The facts about one operand, eight numbers to each in the workspace.)
To 'facts per operand'.
  Return a number, 8.

To 'the form fact'.
  Return a number, 1.

To 'the width fact'.
  Return a number, 2.

(The register's number, or a memory operand's base.)
To 'the register fact'.
  Return a number, 3.

To 'the index fact'.
  Return a number, 4.

To 'the scale fact'.
  Return a number, 5.

(The immediate, or a memory operand's displacement.)
To 'the value fact'.
  Return a number, 6.

(1 when the value is known before any label is placed, 0 when it waits on
 one.)
To 'the known fact'.
  Return a number, 7.

To 'the relative fact'.
  Return a number, 8.

(What an operand is, as the form fact holds it.)
To 'register form'.
  Return a number, 1.

To 'memory form'.
  Return a number, 2.

To 'immediate form'.
  Return a number, 3.

(The number for a memory operand without a base or an index register.)
To 'no register'.
  Return a number, -1.

(The number for a name that is not one of the sixteen 64-bit registers.)
To 'not an address register'.
  Return a number, -2.

(The widths, in bits. An xmm register is a vector of 128.)
To 'vector width'.
  Return a number, 128.

To 'widest operation'.
  Return a number, 64.

(The head of the table is the workspace: the facts about the operands of the
 instruction being encoded, for as many operands as a row can have.)
To 'the workspace size'.
  Return a number, 'facts per operand' multiply 'most operands'.

(Each row of the table takes sixteen numbers: the patterns of its three
 operands, 0 for one it does not have; the last row of its mnemonic; how many
 steps it has; and the steps.)
To 'numbers per row'.
  Return a number, 16.

To 'most operands'.
  Return a number, 3.

To 'the last row slot'.
  Return a number, 4.

To 'the step count slot'.
  Return a number, 5.

To 'the first step slot'.
  Return a number, 6.

To 'most steps'.
  Return a number, 11.

(A pattern is written as one number: its family times 10000, the register a
 fixed register pattern names times 1000, and its width in bits.)
To 'pattern family size'.
  Return a number, 10000.

To 'pattern register size'.
  Return a number, 1000.

To 'register family'.
  Return a number, 1.

To 'register or memory family'.
  Return a number, 2.

To 'memory family'.
  Return a number, 3.

To 'vector or memory family'.
  Return a number, 4.

To 'fixed register family'.
  Return a number, 5.

To 'immediate family'.
  Return a number, 6.

To 'zero-extended immediate family'.
  Return a number, 7.

To 'the number one family'.
  Return a number, 8.

To 'jump target family'.
  Return a number, 9.

(The steps that are not bytes. Every step is one number, laid out so that a
 range says what kind of step it is:
   0 to 255     a byte, its own value
   256          REX.W
   300          /r, the ModRM byte with the register operand
   310 to 317   /0 to /7, the ModRM byte with that digit
   320          +r, the register added to the opcode
   401 to 408   ib, iw, id and iq: 400 and the immediate's bytes
   504          cd, the jump target in four bytes)
To 'the REX W step'.
  Return a number, 256.

To 'the ModRM step'.
  Return a number, 300.

(The ModRM byte with a digit in its reg field is this number and the digit.)
To 'the digit step'.
  Return a number, 310.

To 'the register in the opcode step'.
  Return a number, 320.

(An immediate is this number and how many bytes it takes.)
To 'the immediate step'.
  Return a number, 400.

To 'the jump target step'.
  Return a number, 504.

(The legacy prefixes a row can begin with, which go before the REX prefix.)
To 'operand size prefix'.
  Return a number, 0x66.

To 'repeat or double prefix'.
  Return a number, 0xF2.

To 'repeat or single prefix'.
  Return a number, 0xF3.

To 'the REX base'.
  Return a number, 0x40.

To 'the SIB byte with no index and no base'.
  Return a number, 0x25.

To 'the SIB byte for a base of rsp'.
  Return a number, 0x24.

(--- The table -------------------------------------------------------------)

(Every shape the compiler's output and coreasm use, one row each, in the
 order an instruction is tried against them. A shape with a cheaper form
 nasm prefers also has that form's row in front of it, so the bytes are
 nasm's.)
To 'the encoding rows'.
  a list called rows is [].
  append "adc        | r/m64, imm8          | REX.W 83 /2 ib" to rows.
  append "adc        | rax, imm32           | REX.W 15 id" to rows.
  append "adc        | r/m64, imm32         | REX.W 81 /2 id" to rows.
  append "adc        | r/m64, r64           | REX.W 11 /r" to rows.
  append "adc        | r64, r/m64           | REX.W 13 /r" to rows.
  append "add        | al, imm8             | 04 ib" to rows.
  append "add        | r/m8, imm8           | 80 /0 ib" to rows.
  append "add        | r/m64, imm8          | REX.W 83 /0 ib" to rows.
  append "add        | rax, imm32           | REX.W 05 id" to rows.
  append "add        | r/m64, imm32         | REX.W 81 /0 id" to rows.
  append "add        | r/m64, r64           | REX.W 01 /r" to rows.
  append "add        | r64, r/m64           | REX.W 03 /r" to rows.
  append "addsd      | xmm, xmm/m64         | F2 0F 58 /r" to rows.
  append "and        | al, imm8             | 24 ib" to rows.
  append "and        | r/m8, imm8           | 80 /4 ib" to rows.
