(--- Reading the table -----------------------------------------------------)

(The words of the rows, with the number each stands for. Only reading the
 table uses them, once, so they can live in a map.)
To 'learn the encoder words' with a map called vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "r8" and {'register family'} and 0 and 8 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "r16" and {'register family'} and 0 and 16 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "r32" and {'register family'} and 0 and 32 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "r64" and {'register family'} and 0 and 64 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "xmm" and {'register family'} and 0 and {'vector width'} and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "r/m8" and {'register or memory family'} and 0 and 8 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "r/m16" and {'register or memory family'} and 0 and 16 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "r/m32" and {'register or memory family'} and 0 and 32 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "r/m64" and {'register or memory family'} and 0 and 64 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "m" and {'memory family'} and 0 and 0 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "m16" and {'memory family'} and 0 and 16 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "m64" and {'memory family'} and 0 and 64 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "xmm/m64" and {'vector or memory family'} and 0 and 64 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "al" and {'fixed register family'} and 0 and 8 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "cl" and {'fixed register family'} and 1 and 8 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "ax" and {'fixed register family'} and 0 and 16 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "eax" and {'fixed register family'} and 0 and 32 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "rax" and {'fixed register family'} and 0 and 64 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "1" and {'the number one family'} and 0 and 0 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "imm8" and {'immediate family'} and 0 and 8 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "imm16" and {'immediate family'} and 0 and 16 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "imm32" and {'immediate family'} and 0 and 32 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "imm64" and {'immediate family'} and 0 and 64 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "imm32u" and {'zero-extended immediate family'} and 0 and 32 and vocabulary.
  'learn the pattern' with "rel32" and {'jump target family'} and 0 and 32 and vocabulary.
  Set vocabulary's "step REX.W" to 'the REX W step'.
  Set vocabulary's "step /r" to 'the ModRM step'.
  For each digit from 0 to 7,
    Set vocabulary's "step /{digit}" to 'the digit step' add digit.
  Set vocabulary's "step +r" to 'the register in the opcode step'.
  Set vocabulary's "step ib" to 'the immediate step' add 1.
  Set vocabulary's "step iw" to 'the immediate step' add 2.
  Set vocabulary's "step id" to 'the immediate step' add 4.
  Set vocabulary's "step iq" to 'the immediate step' add 8.
  Set vocabulary's "step cd" to 'the jump target step'.

To 'learn the pattern' with a text called word and a number called family and a number called 'fixed register' and a number called width and a map called vocabulary.
  a number called pattern is family multiply 'pattern family size'.
  Set pattern to pattern add {'fixed register' multiply 'pattern register size'} add width.
  Set vocabulary's "pattern {word}" to pattern.

(Read every row into the table: first the workspace, then sixteen numbers to
 a row, as the named numbers above lay them out, with each row's mnemonic on
 the row mnemonics list. A row the encoder cannot read is a problem with the
 table itself.)
To 'prepare the encoder' with a list called table and a list called 'row mnemonics'.
  a map called vocabulary is {}.
  'learn the encoder words' with vocabulary.
  a list called rows is 'the encoding rows'.
  a buffer called problems.
  Repeat 'the workspace size' times, append 0 to table.
  For each 'row number' from 1 to rows's length,
    a text called row is element 'row number' of rows,
    a list called fields is 'the pieces of the row' with row and '|' and true,
    a text called mnemonic is element 1 of fields,
    if fields's length is not 3 then, append "row {row number} does not have three fields: {row}\n" to problems.
    if {'the rows of the mnemonic are apart' with mnemonic and 'row mnemonics'} then, append "the rows of {mnemonic} do not stand together\n" to problems.
    append mnemonic to 'row mnemonics',
    'read the row' with fields and 'row number' and table and vocabulary and problems.
  'mark the last rows' with table and 'row mnemonics'.
  Return a text, problems as text.

(A mnemonic's rows stand together, so a row can end the search for an
 instruction's row: a mnemonic seen before, but not on the row just before
 this one, is out of place.)
To 'the rows of the mnemonic are apart' with a text called mnemonic and a list called 'row mnemonics'.
  a number called 'rows so far' is 'row mnemonics''s length.
  If 'rows so far' is 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
  If {element 'rows so far' of 'row mnemonics'} is mnemonic then, Return a boolean, false.
  Return a boolean, {'the first row of the mnemonic' with mnemonic and 'row mnemonics'} is greater than 0.

(Each row learns the last row of its mnemonic. The mnemonic is named before
 it is compared: two elements of a list parameter compared with each other
 directly come out unequal even when they are the same text, which is Vox
 bug #157.)
To 'mark the last rows' with a list called table and a list called 'row mnemonics'.
  a number called 'last row' is 'row mnemonics''s length.
  a number called 'row number' is 'row mnemonics''s length.
  While 'row number' is greater than 0,
    set element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and 'the last row slot'} of table to 'last row',
    a text called mnemonic is element 'row number' of 'row mnemonics',
    if 'row number' is greater than 1 then, if {element {'row number' subtract 1} of 'row mnemonics'} is not mnemonic then, set 'last row' to 'row number' subtract 1..
    decrement 'row number'.

(Where a row's slot stands in the table, past the workspace.)
To 'the row slot' with a number called 'row number' and a number called slot.
  Return a number, 'the workspace size' add {{'row number' subtract 1} multiply 'numbers per row'} add slot.

(The first row of a mnemonic, found by reading down the row mnemonics, or 0
 when the table has none. Comparing texts makes nothing, where a map key put
 together as text would.)
To 'the first row of the mnemonic' with a text called mnemonic and a list called 'row mnemonics'.
  For each 'row number' from 1 to 'row mnemonics''s length,
    if {element 'row number' of 'row mnemonics'} is mnemonic then, Return a number, 'row number'..
  Return a number, 0.

(One row: its operand patterns, then its steps.)
To 'read the row' with a list called fields and a number called 'row number' and a list called table and a map called vocabulary and a buffer called problems.
  Repeat 'numbers per row' times, append 0 to table.
  If fields's length is not 3 then, Return.
  a text called 'operand field' is element 2 of fields.
  a text called 'step field' is element 3 of fields.
  a list called patterns is 'the pieces of the row' with 'operand field' and ',' and false.
  a list called steps is 'the pieces of the row' with 'step field' and ' ' and false.
  If patterns's length is greater than 'most operands' then, append "row {row number} has too many operands\n" to problems, Return.
  If steps's length is greater than 'most steps' then, append "row {row number} has too many steps\n" to problems, Return.
  For each 'operand number' from 1 to patterns's length,
    a text called 'pattern word' is element 'operand number' of patterns,
    a number called pattern is vocabulary's "pattern {pattern word}",
    if pattern is 0 then, append "row {row number} has the unknown operand pattern {pattern word}\n" to problems.
    set element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and 'operand number'} of table to pattern.
  set element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and 'the step count slot'} of table to steps's length.
  For each 'step number' from 1 to steps's length,
    a text called 'step word' is element 'step number' of steps,
    a number called step is 'the step the word stands for' with 'step word' and vocabulary,
    if step is less than 0 then, append "row {row number} has the unknown step {step word}\n" to problems.
    set element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and {'the first step slot' add 'step number' subtract 1}} of table to step.

(A step word: one of the named steps, or two hex digits for a byte. -1 for a
 word that is neither.)
To 'the step the word stands for' with a text called word and a map called vocabulary.
  a number called step is vocabulary's "step {word}".
  If step is greater than 0 then, Return a number, step.
  a buffer called characters is "{word}".
  If characters's size is not 2 then, Return a number, -1.
  a number called 'byte value' is -1.
  Set 'byte value' to word as a hex number.
  On error set 'byte value' to -1.
  Return a number, 'byte value'.

(The pieces of a text between separators, with the spaces at each end of a
 piece taken off. An empty piece is kept only when asked for, so that a row's
 empty operand field still counts as a field.)
To 'the pieces of the row' with a text called written and a number called separator and a boolean called 'keeping empty pieces'.
  a buffer called characters is "{written}".
  a list called pieces is [].
  a buffer called piece.
  a number called 'spaces held back' is 0.
  For each position from 1 to characters's size,
    a number called character is byte position of characters,
    a boolean called 'it ends the piece' is false,
    if character is separator then, set 'it ends the piece' to true.
    a boolean called 'it is a space' is false,
    if character is ' ' then, set 'it is a space' to true.
    if 'it ends the piece' then, 'keep the piece' with piece and pieces and 'keeping empty pieces', set 'spaces held back' to 0.
    if 'it ends the piece' is false and 'it is a space' and piece's size is greater than 0 then, increment 'spaces held back'.
    if 'it ends the piece' is false and 'it is a space' is false then, 'add the spaces held back' with 'spaces held back' and piece, set 'spaces held back' to 0, 'add the byte to' with character and piece..
  'keep the piece' with piece and pieces and 'keeping empty pieces'.
  Return a list, pieces.

To 'keep the piece' with a buffer called piece and a list called pieces and a boolean called 'keeping empty pieces'.
  If piece's size is 0 and 'keeping empty pieces' is false then, Return.
  a text called 'finished piece' is piece as text.
  append 'finished piece' to pieces.
  clear piece.

To 'add the spaces held back' with a number called 'spaces held back' and a buffer called piece.
  Repeat 'spaces held back' times, 'add the byte to' with ' ' and piece.

To 'add the byte to' with a number called 'byte value' and a buffer called output.
  Set byte {output's size add 1} of output to 'byte value'.

(--- The facts about the operands ------------------------------------------)

(Each operand's record, read once into eight numbers in the workspace: its
 form, its width in bits, its register or base, its index, its scale, its
 value or displacement, whether that value is known before any label is
 placed, and whether the memory is relative to the instruction pointer.
 Answers what is wrong with an operand, or the empty text.

 The workspace is the head of the table, so the functions here, which touch
 only the workspace, call the list workspace; the functions after them read
 both the rows and the facts from the one list, and call it table.)
To 'gather the facts' with a list called operands and a list called workspace.
  For each 'workspace slot' from 1 to 'the workspace size',
    set element 'workspace slot' of workspace to 0.
  If operands's length is greater than 'most operands' then, Return a text, "an instruction with more than three operands cannot be encoded".
  For each 'operand number' from 1 to operands's length,
    a map called operand is element 'operand number' of operands,
    a text called kind is operand's "kind",
    a number called 'operand workspace start' is {'operand number' subtract 1} multiply 'facts per operand',
    a text called problem is "",
    if kind is "register" then, set problem to 'gather the register facts' with operand and 'operand workspace start' and workspace.
    if kind is "memory" then, set problem to 'gather the memory facts' with operand and 'operand workspace start' and workspace.
    if kind is "expression" then, set problem to 'gather the immediate facts' with operand and 'operand workspace start' and workspace.
    if kind is not "register" and kind is not "memory" and kind is not "expression" then, set problem to "an operand of the kind {kind} cannot be encoded".
    if problem is not "" then, Return a text, problem..
  Return a text, "".

To 'gather the register facts' with a map called operand and a number called 'operand workspace start' and a list called workspace.
  a text called class is operand's "class".
  a number called width is 'the width of the register class' of class.
  If width is 0 then, Return a text, "a register of the class {class} cannot be encoded".
  a number called 'register number' is operand's "number".
  Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the form fact'} of workspace to 'register form'.
  Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the width fact'} of workspace to width.
  Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the register fact'} of workspace to 'register number'.
  Return a text, "".

To 'the width of the register class' of a text called class.
  If class is "r64" then, Return a number, 64.
  If class is "r32" then, Return a number, 32.
  If class is "r16" then, Return a number, 16.
  If class is "r8" then, Return a number, 8.
  If class is "xmm" then, Return a number, 'vector width'.
  Return a number, 0.

(A memory operand's size word, in bits; 0 for memory written without one.)
To 'the width of the size word' of a text called 'size word'.
  If 'size word' is "byte" then, Return a number, 8.
  If 'size word' is "word" then, Return a number, 16.
  If 'size word' is "dword" then, Return a number, 32.
  If 'size word' is "qword" then, Return a number, 64.
  Return a number, 0.

To 'gather the memory facts' with a map called operand and a number called 'operand workspace start' and a list called workspace.
  a text called 'size word' is operand's "size".
  a text called base is operand's "base".
  a text called 'index register' is operand's "index".
  a number called scale is operand's "scale".
  a number called displacement is operand's "displacement value".
  a boolean called 'it is known' is operand's "displacement is constant".
  a boolean called 'it is relative' is operand's "relative".
  a number called 'base number' is 'the number of the address register' of base.
  a number called 'index number' is 'the number of the address register' of 'index register'.
  If 'base number' is 'not an address register' then, Return a text, "memory cannot be addressed through {base}".
  If 'index number' is 'not an address register' then, Return a text, "memory cannot be addressed through {index register}".
  If 'index number' is 4 then, Return a text, "rsp cannot be the index register of a memory operand".
  If 'index number' is not 'no register' and scale is not 1 and scale is not 2 and scale is not 4 and scale is not 8 then, Return a text, "the scale of an index register must be 1, 2, 4 or 8".
  Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the form fact'} of workspace to 'memory form'.
  Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the width fact'} of workspace to 'the width of the size word' of 'size word'.
  Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the register fact'} of workspace to 'base number'.
  Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the index fact'} of workspace to 'index number'.
  Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the scale fact'} of workspace to scale.
  Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the value fact'} of workspace to displacement.
  If 'it is known' then, Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the known fact'} of workspace to 1.
  If 'it is relative' then, Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the relative fact'} of workspace to 1.
  Return a text, "".

(The number of a register a memory operand names, in the order the
 processor numbers them: no register for none, and not an address register
 for a name that is not one of the sixteen 64-bit registers.)
To 'the number of the address register' of a text called 'register name'.
  If 'register name' is "" then, Return a number, 'no register'.
  If 'register name' is "rax" then, Return a number, 0.
  If 'register name' is "rcx" then, Return a number, 1.
  If 'register name' is "rdx" then, Return a number, 2.
  If 'register name' is "rbx" then, Return a number, 3.
  If 'register name' is "rsp" then, Return a number, 4.
  If 'register name' is "rbp" then, Return a number, 5.
  If 'register name' is "rsi" then, Return a number, 6.
  If 'register name' is "rdi" then, Return a number, 7.
  If 'register name' is "r8" then, Return a number, 8.
  If 'register name' is "r9" then, Return a number, 9.
  If 'register name' is "r10" then, Return a number, 10.
  If 'register name' is "r11" then, Return a number, 11.
  If 'register name' is "r12" then, Return a number, 12.
  If 'register name' is "r13" then, Return a number, 13.
  If 'register name' is "r14" then, Return a number, 14.
  If 'register name' is "r15" then, Return a number, 15.
  Return a number, 'not an address register'.

To 'gather the immediate facts' with a map called operand and a number called 'operand workspace start' and a list called workspace.
  a number called value is operand's "value".
  a boolean called 'it is known' is operand's "is constant".
  Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the form fact'} of workspace to 'immediate form'.
  Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the value fact'} of workspace to value.
  If 'it is known' then, Set element {'operand workspace start' add 'the known fact'} of workspace to 1.
  Return a text, "".

(One fact about one operand, from the workspace.)
To 'the fact about the operand' with a list called workspace and a number called operand and a number called fact.
  Return a number, element {{operand subtract 1} multiply 'facts per operand' add fact} of workspace.

(--- Choosing the row ------------------------------------------------------)

To 'the family of the pattern' of a number called pattern.
  Return a number, pattern divide 'pattern family size'.

To 'the width of the pattern' of a number called pattern.
  Return a number, pattern modulo 'pattern register size'.

To 'the register the pattern names' of a number called pattern.
  Return a number, pattern modulo 'pattern family size' divide 'pattern register size'.

(The row of the table an instruction fits, or 0 for none: the first of its
 mnemonic's rows whose patterns all its operands fit.)
To 'the row for the operands' with a number called 'first row' and a number called 'operand count' and a list called table.
  a number called 'last row' is element {'the row slot' with 'first row' and 'the last row slot'} of table.
  For each 'row number' from 'first row' to 'last row',
    if 'the row fits' with 'row number' and 'operand count' and table then, Return a number, 'row number'..
  Return a number, 0.

To 'the row fits' with a number called 'row number' and a number called 'operand count' and a list called table.
  a number called 'operation width' is 'the operation width of the row' with 'row number' and table.
  a number called 'register width' is 'the register width of the row' with 'row number' and table.
  For each operand from 1 to 'most operands',
    a number called pattern is element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and operand} of table,
    if operand is greater than 'operand count' and pattern is not 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
    if operand is not greater than 'operand count' and pattern is 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
    if pattern is not 0 and {'the operand fits the pattern' with table and operand and pattern and 'operation width' and 'register width'} is false then, Return a boolean, false..
  Return a boolean, true.

(How wide the operation is, which decides how an immediate is extended: the
 width of the first operand, a byte beside an xmm register, and the full 64
 bits when the immediate stands alone.)
To 'the operation width of the row' with a number called 'row number' and a list called table.
  a number called pattern is element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and 1} of table.
  a number called family is 'the family of the pattern' of pattern.
  a number called width is 'the width of the pattern' of pattern.
  If family is greater than 'fixed register family' or width is 0 then, Return a number, 'widest operation'.
  If width is 'vector width' then, Return a number, 8.
  Return a number, width.

(The width a register pattern in the row gives the operation, which is what
 lets memory written without a size word fit an r/m pattern; 0 when the row
 has no such register.)
To 'the register width of the row' with a number called 'row number' and a list called table.
  For each operand from 1 to 'most operands',
    a number called pattern is element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and operand} of table,
    a number called family is 'the family of the pattern' of pattern,
    a number called width is 'the width of the pattern' of pattern,
    if family is 'register family' or family is 'fixed register family' then, if width is not 'vector width' then, Return a number, width...
  Return a number, 0.

To 'the operand fits the pattern' with a list called table and a number called operand and a number called pattern and a number called 'operation width' and a number called 'register width'.
  a number called family is 'the family of the pattern' of pattern.
  a number called width is 'the width of the pattern' of pattern.
  a number called form is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the form fact'.
  a number called 'operand width' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the width fact'.
  a boolean called 'it is a register' is false.
  If form is 'register form' then, set 'it is a register' to true.
  a boolean called 'it is memory' is false.
  If form is 'memory form' then, set 'it is memory' to true.
  a boolean called 'the size fits' is false.
  If 'operand width' is width then, set 'the size fits' to true.
  If family is 'register family' then, Return a boolean, 'it is a register' and 'the size fits'.
  If family is 'register or memory family' then, Return a boolean, 'the register or memory fits' with 'it is a register' and 'it is memory' and 'operand width' and width and 'register width'.
  If family is 'memory family' then, Return a boolean, 'the memory fits' with 'it is memory' and 'operand width' and width.
  If family is 'vector or memory family' then, Return a boolean, 'the vector or memory fits' with 'it is a register' and 'it is memory' and 'operand width' and width.
  If family is 'fixed register family' then, Return a boolean, 'the fixed register fits' with table and operand and 'it is a register' and 'the size fits' and pattern.
  If form is not 'immediate form' then, Return a boolean, false.
  If family is 'jump target family' then, Return a boolean, true.
  a number called value is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the value fact'.
  a boolean called 'it is known' is false.
  If {'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the known fact'} is 1 then, set 'it is known' to true.
  If family is 'the number one family' then, Return a boolean, 'it is known' and value is 1.
  If family is 'zero-extended immediate family' then, Return a boolean, 'the number fits zero-extended' with value and 'it is known' and width.
  If family is 'immediate family' then, Return a boolean, 'the number fits sign-extended' with value and 'it is known' and width and 'operation width'.
  Return a boolean, false.

(Memory written without a size word fits only where a register of the row
 gives it the size.)
To 'the register or memory fits' with a boolean called 'it is a register' and a boolean called 'it is memory' and a number called 'operand width' and a number called width and a number called 'register width'.
  If 'operand width' is width then, Return a boolean, 'it is a register' or 'it is memory'.
  If 'it is memory' and 'operand width' is 0 and 'register width' is width then, Return a boolean, true.
  Return a boolean, false.

To 'the memory fits' with a boolean called 'it is memory' and a number called 'operand width' and a number called width.
  If 'it is memory' is false then, Return a boolean, false.
  If width is 0 or 'operand width' is 0 or 'operand width' is width then, Return a boolean, true.
  Return a boolean, false.

To 'the vector or memory fits' with a boolean called 'it is a register' and a boolean called 'it is memory' and a number called 'operand width' and a number called width.
  If 'it is a register' and 'operand width' is 'vector width' then, Return a boolean, true.
  If 'it is memory' and 'operand width' is 0 then, Return a boolean, true.
  If 'it is memory' and 'operand width' is width then, Return a boolean, true.
  Return a boolean, false.

To 'the fixed register fits' with a list called table and a number called operand and a boolean called 'it is a register' and a boolean called 'the size fits' and a number called pattern.
  If 'it is a register' is false or 'the size fits' is false then, Return a boolean, false.
  a number called 'register number' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the register fact'.
  Return a boolean, 'register number' is 'the register the pattern names' of pattern.

(A number fits an immediate of a width when the processor, extending it by
 its sign to the width of the operation, gets the number back. A number that
 waits on a label is taken at the full width of the operation.)
To 'the number fits sign-extended' with a number called value and a boolean called 'it is known' and a number called width and a number called 'operation width'.
  If 'it is known' is false then, Return a boolean, width is greater than or equal to 'operation width'.
  If 'operation width' is greater than or equal to 'widest operation' then, Return a boolean, 'the number fits signed' with value and width.
  a number called 'operation span' is 1 bit-shift-left 'operation width'.
  a number called 'half operation span' is 1 bit-shift-left {'operation width' subtract 1}.
  If value is less than 0 subtract 'half operation span' then, Return a boolean, false.
  If value is greater than or equal to 'operation span' then, Return a boolean, false.
  If width is greater than or equal to 'operation width' then, Return a boolean, true.
  a number called truncated is value bit-and {'operation span' subtract 1}.
  a number called 'half span' is 1 bit-shift-left {width subtract 1}.
  If truncated is less than 'half span' then, Return a boolean, true.
  Return a boolean, truncated is greater than or equal to 'operation span' subtract 'half span'.

(Whether a whole 64-bit number is one the width holds as a signed number.)
To 'the number fits signed' with a number called value and a number called width.
  If width is greater than or equal to 'widest operation' then, Return a boolean, true.
  a number called 'half span' is 1 bit-shift-left {width subtract 1}.
  If value is less than 0 subtract 'half span' then, Return a boolean, false.
  Return a boolean, value is less than 'half span'.

(A 32-bit immediate the processor extends with zeros, as a 32-bit mov does
 into its 64-bit register.)
To 'the number fits zero-extended' with a number called value and a boolean called 'it is known' and a number called width.
  If 'it is known' is false then, Return a boolean, false.
  If value is less than 0 then, Return a boolean, false.
  Return a boolean, value is less than {1 bit-shift-left width}.

(--- Laying down the bytes -------------------------------------------------)

(The bytes of one instruction, appended to the output, laid down at the
 address. Until the addresses are final, a field measured from the end of
 the instruction is left as zeros and its reach is not checked, so the size
 comes out the same whatever the labels hold.)
To 'encode the record' with a map called record and a number called address and a boolean called 'the addresses are final' and a list called table and a list called 'row mnemonics' and a buffer called output.
  a text called mnemonic is record's "mnemonic".
  a number called 'first row' is 'the first row of the mnemonic' with mnemonic and 'row mnemonics'.
  If 'first row' is 0 then, Return a text, 'the message for the unknown mnemonic' with mnemonic.
  a list called operands is record's "operands".
  a text called problem is 'gather the facts' with operands and table.
  If problem is not "" then, Return a text, problem.
  a number called 'operand count' is operands's length.
  a number called 'row number' is 'the row for the operands' with 'first row' and 'operand count' and table.
  If 'row number' is 0 then, Return a text, 'the message for the unknown shape' with record.
  Return a text, 'lay down the row' with 'row number' and 'operand count' and address and 'the addresses are final' and table and output.

To 'the message for the unknown mnemonic' with a text called mnemonic.
  a text called complaint is "".
  Set complaint to "vas has no encoding for the instruction {mnemonic}".
  Return a text, complaint.

To 'the message for the unknown shape' with a map called record.
  a text called written is record's "text".
  a text called complaint is "".
  Set complaint to "vas has no encoding for these operands: {written}".
  Return a text, complaint.

(Which operand each part of the row takes: the addressed operand goes in the
 r/m field of the ModRM byte, the register operand in its reg field or the
 opcode, then the immediate and the jump target. 0 for a part the row does
 not have. A row with a ModRM byte and no r/m pattern, the two-operand imul,
 addresses its register operand too.)
To 'the operand in the role' with a number called 'row number' and a number called role and a list called table.
  For each operand from 1 to 'most operands',
    a number called pattern is element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and operand} of table,
    a number called family is 'the family of the pattern' of pattern,
    if pattern is not 0 and {'the role of the family' of family} is role then, Return a number, operand..
  Return a number, 0.

To 'the addressed role'.
  Return a number, 1.

To 'the register role'.
  Return a number, 2.

To 'the immediate role'.
  Return a number, 3.

To 'the role of the family' of a number called family.
  If family is 'register or memory family' or family is 'memory family' or family is 'vector or memory family' then, Return a number, 'the addressed role'.
  If family is 'register family' then, Return a number, 'the register role'.
  If family is 'immediate family' or family is 'zero-extended immediate family' or family is 'jump target family' then, Return a number, 'the immediate role'.
  Return a number, 0.

To 'lay down the row' with a number called 'row number' and a number called 'operand count' and a number called address and a boolean called 'the addresses are final' and a list called table and a buffer called output.
  a number called 'addressed operand' is 'the operand in the role' with 'row number' and 'the addressed role' and table.
  a number called 'register operand' is 'the operand in the role' with 'row number' and 'the register role' and table.
  a number called 'immediate operand' is 'the operand in the role' with 'row number' and 'the immediate role' and table.
  If 'addressed operand' is 0 then, set 'addressed operand' to 'register operand'.
  a number called 'step count' is element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and 'the step count slot'} of table.
  a number called 'REX byte' is 'the REX prefix' with 'row number' and 'operand count' and 'addressed operand' and 'register operand' and table.
  a boolean called 'the REX byte is laid down' is false.
  If 'REX byte' is 0 then, set 'the REX byte is laid down' to true.
  a number called 'instruction start' is output's size.
  (A field measured from the end of the instruction, a jump's target or
   memory relative to the instruction pointer, is laid down as zeros and
   written once the end is known: where it begins, and the operand whose
   value is its target.)
  a number called 'field position' is 0.
  a number called 'field operand' is 0.
  For each 'step number' from 1 to 'step count',
    a number called step is element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and {'the first step slot' add 'step number' subtract 1}} of table,
    a boolean called 'it is a ModRM step' is false,
    if step is 'the ModRM step' or {'the step is a digit' of step} then, set 'it is a ModRM step' to true.
    if step is less than 256 and 'the REX byte is laid down' is false and {'the byte is a legacy prefix' of step} is false then, 'add the byte to' with 'REX byte' and output, set 'the REX byte is laid down' to true.
    if step is less than 256 then, 'add the byte to' with step and output.
    if step is 'the register in the opcode step' then, 'add the register to the opcode' with {'the fact about the operand' with table and 'register operand' and 'the register fact'} and output.
    if 'it is a ModRM step' then, set 'field position' to 'lay down the address' with {'the register field' with step and table and 'register operand'} and table and 'addressed operand' and output.
    if 'it is a ModRM step' and 'field position' is greater than 0 then, set 'field operand' to 'addressed operand'.
    if step is greater than 'the immediate step' and step is less than 'the jump target step' then, 'lay down the number' with {'the fact about the operand' with table and 'immediate operand' and 'the value fact'} and {step subtract 'the immediate step'} and output.
    if step is 'the jump target step' then, set 'field position' to 'lay down the jump target' with output, set 'field operand' to 'immediate operand'..
  If 'field position' is 0 then, Return a text, "".
  a number called 'field target' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and 'field operand' and 'the value fact'.
  a number called 'next address' is address add output's size subtract 'instruction start'.
  a number called distance is 'field target' subtract 'next address'.
  If 'the addresses are final' is false then, Return a text, "".
  If {'the number fits signed' with distance and 32} is false then, Return a text, "the target is too far from the instruction to reach in 32 bits".
  'write the number' with distance and 4 and 'field position' and output.
  Return a text, "".

To 'the byte is a legacy prefix' of a number called step.
  If step is 'operand size prefix' or step is 'repeat or double prefix' or step is 'repeat or single prefix' then, Return a boolean, true.
  Return a boolean, false.

To 'the step is a digit' of a number called step.
  If step is less than 'the digit step' then, Return a boolean, false.
  Return a boolean, step is less than 'the digit step' add 8.

(A jump's target, as four zeros until the end of the instruction is known.
 Answers where the four bytes begin in the output.)
To 'lay down the jump target' with a buffer called output.
  a number called 'field position' is output's size add 1.
  'lay down the number' with 0 and 4 and output.
  Return a number, 'field position'.

(The reg field of the ModRM byte: the digit the row names, or the low three
 bits of the register operand.)
To 'the register field' with a number called step and a list called table and a number called 'register operand'.
  If {'the step is a digit' of step} then, Return a number, step subtract 'the digit step'.
  a number called 'register number' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and 'register operand' and 'the register fact'.
  Return a number, 'register number' bit-and 7.

To 'add the register to the opcode' with a number called 'register number' and a buffer called output.
  a number called opcode is byte {output's size} of output.
  Set byte {output's size} of output to opcode add {'register number' bit-and 7}.

(The REX prefix the instruction needs, or 0 for none. W comes from the row;
 R, X and B carry the fourth bit of the reg field, the index and the base or
 r/m field; and a byte register numbered 4 to 7 is spl, bpl, sil or dil,
 which only a REX prefix reaches.)
To 'the REX prefix' with a number called 'row number' and a number called 'operand count' and a number called 'addressed operand' and a number called 'register operand' and a list called table.
  a number called 'REX bits' is 0.
  a boolean called 'it is needed' is false.
  a number called 'step count' is element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and 'the step count slot'} of table.
  a boolean called 'it has a ModRM byte' is false.
  a boolean called 'it adds the register to the opcode' is false.
  For each 'step number' from 1 to 'step count',
    a number called step is element {'the row slot' with 'row number' and {'the first step slot' add 'step number' subtract 1}} of table,
    if step is 'the REX W step' then, set 'REX bits' to 'REX bits' bit-or 8, set 'it is needed' to true.
    if step is 'the ModRM step' then, set 'it has a ModRM byte' to true.
    if step is 'the register in the opcode step' then, set 'it adds the register to the opcode' to true..
  a boolean called 'the register is numbered past 7' is false.
  If 'register operand' is greater than 0 then, if {'the fact about the operand' with table and 'register operand' and 'the register fact'} is greater than 7 then, set 'the register is numbered past 7' to true..
  If 'the register is numbered past 7' and 'it has a ModRM byte' then, set 'REX bits' to 'REX bits' bit-or 4.
  If 'the register is numbered past 7' and 'it adds the register to the opcode' then, set 'REX bits' to 'REX bits' bit-or 1.
  If 'addressed operand' is greater than 0 then, set 'REX bits' to 'REX bits' bit-or {'the REX bits of the address' with table and 'addressed operand'}.
  If 'REX bits' is greater than 0 then, set 'it is needed' to true.
  For each operand from 1 to 'operand count',
    a number called form is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the form fact',
    a number called width is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the width fact',
    a number called 'register number' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the register fact',
    if form is 'register form' and width is 8 and {'the register needs a REX prefix' of 'register number'} then, set 'it is needed' to true..
  If 'it is needed' is false then, Return a number, 0.
  Return a number, 'the REX base' bit-or 'REX bits'.

(spl, bpl, sil and dil share their numbers with ah, ch, dh and bh, which an
 instruction without a REX prefix reaches instead.)
To 'the register needs a REX prefix' of a number called 'register number'.
  If 'register number' is less than 4 then, Return a boolean, false.
  Return a boolean, 'register number' is less than 8.

(X and B for the addressed operand: B for a register or a base numbered 8
 to 15, X for such an index.)
To 'the REX bits of the address' with a list called table and a number called operand.
  a number called bits is 0.
  a number called form is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the form fact'.
  a number called 'base number' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the register fact'.
  a number called 'index number' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the index fact'.
  If 'base number' is greater than 7 then, set bits to bits bit-or 1.
  If form is 'memory form' and 'index number' is greater than 7 then, set bits to bits bit-or 2.
  Return a number, bits.

(The ModRM byte for the addressed operand, with any SIB byte and
 displacement after it. Answers where a displacement measured from the end of
 the instruction begins in the output, for memory relative to the
 instruction pointer, or 0.)
To 'lay down the address' with a number called 'register field' and a list called table and a number called operand and a buffer called output.
  a number called form is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the form fact'.
  a number called 'base number' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the register fact'.
  a number called 'register field bits' is 'register field' multiply 8.
  If form is 'register form' then, 'add the byte to' with {0xC0 add 'register field bits' add {'base number' bit-and 7}} and output, Return a number, 0.
  a number called 'index number' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the index fact'.
  a number called displacement is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the value fact'.
  a boolean called 'there is a base' is true.
  If 'base number' is 'no register' then, set 'there is a base' to false.
  a boolean called 'there is an index' is true.
  If 'index number' is 'no register' then, set 'there is an index' to false.
  If 'there is a base' is false and 'there is an index' is false then, Return a number, 'lay down the address without registers' with 'register field bits' and table and operand and output.
  a number called 'displacement size' is 'the displacement size' with table and operand.
  a number called 'mod field bits' is 'the mod field bits for the displacement size' of 'displacement size'.
  If 'there is a base' is false then, set 'mod field bits' to 0.
  If 'there is an index' is false and {'base number' bit-and 7} is not 4 then, 'add the byte to' with {'mod field bits' add 'register field bits' add {'base number' bit-and 7}} and output.
  If 'there is an index' is false and {'base number' bit-and 7} is 4 then, 'add the byte to' with {'mod field bits' add 'register field bits' add 4} and output, 'add the byte to' with 'the SIB byte for a base of rsp' and output.
  If 'there is an index' then, 'add the byte to' with {'mod field bits' add 'register field bits' add 4} and output, 'add the byte to' with {'the SIB byte' with table and operand} and output.
  'lay down the number' with displacement and 'displacement size' and output.
  Return a number, 0.

(Memory with no register: relative to the instruction pointer, a ModRM r/m
 of 5 and a displacement filled in once the end of the instruction is
 known; or an absolute address, a SIB byte with neither index nor base and
 the address in 32 bits.)
To 'lay down the address without registers' with a number called 'register field bits' and a list called table and a number called operand and a buffer called output.
  a number called displacement is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the value fact'.
  If {'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the relative fact'} is 1 then, 'add the byte to' with {'register field bits' add 5} and output, 'lay down the number' with 0 and 4 and output, Return a number, output's size subtract 3.
  'add the byte to' with {'register field bits' add 4} and output.
  'add the byte to' with 'the SIB byte with no index and no base' and output.
  'lay down the number' with displacement and 4 and output.
  Return a number, 0.

(How many bytes the displacement takes: none for 0, unless the base is rbp
 or r13, whose r/m bits with no displacement mean something else, so they
 take a byte of 0; one byte for a number that fits; four otherwise, and
 always four for a displacement that waits on a label or has no base.)
To 'the displacement size' with a list called table and a number called operand.
  a number called 'base number' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the register fact'.
  a number called displacement is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the value fact'.
  a boolean called 'it waits on a label' is false.
  If {'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the known fact'} is 0 then, set 'it waits on a label' to true.
  If 'base number' is 'no register' or 'it waits on a label' then, Return a number, 4.
  If displacement is 0 and {'base number' bit-and 7} is not 5 then, Return a number, 0.
  If {'the number fits signed' with displacement and 8} then, Return a number, 1.
  Return a number, 4.

To 'the mod field bits for the displacement size' of a number called 'displacement size'.
  If 'displacement size' is 1 then, Return a number, 0x40.
  If 'displacement size' is 4 then, Return a number, 0x80.
  Return a number, 0.

(The SIB byte: the scale as a power of two, the index, and the base, or 5
 for none.)
To 'the SIB byte' with a list called table and a number called operand.
  a number called 'base number' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the register fact'.
  a number called 'index number' is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the index fact'.
  a number called scale is 'the fact about the operand' with table and operand and 'the scale fact'.
  a number called 'scale bits' is 0.
  If scale is 2 then, set 'scale bits' to 0x40.
  If scale is 4 then, set 'scale bits' to 0x80.
  If scale is 8 then, set 'scale bits' to 0xC0.
  a number called 'base bits' is 5.
  If 'base number' is not 'no register' then, set 'base bits' to 'base number' bit-and 7.
  Return a number, 'scale bits' add {{'index number' bit-and 7} multiply 8} add 'base bits'.

(A number in that many bytes, least significant first, as x86 keeps it. A
 negative number comes out in two's complement, since the shift brings in
 zeros and the mask keeps the low byte.)
To 'lay down the number' with a number called value and a number called 'byte count' and a buffer called output.
  For each 'byte number' from 1 to 'byte count',
    a number called shift is {'byte number' subtract 1} multiply 8,
    'add the byte to' with {value bit-shift-right shift bit-and 255} and output.

(The same, written over bytes already laid down, from the position given.)
To 'write the number' with a number called value and a number called 'byte count' and a number called position and a buffer called output.
  For each 'byte number' from 1 to 'byte count',
    a number called shift is {'byte number' subtract 1} multiply 8,
    Set byte {position add 'byte number' subtract 1} of output to value bit-shift-right shift bit-and 255.

(--- The interface ---------------------------------------------------------)

(The instruction's bytes, appended to the output, laid down at the address.
 Answers what is wrong with the instruction, or the empty text; nothing is
 appended then.)
To 'encode the instruction' with a map called record and a number called address and a list called table and a list called 'row mnemonics' and a buffer called output.
  a number called 'instruction start' is output's size.
  a text called problem is 'encode the record' with record and address and true and table and 'row mnemonics' and output.
  If problem is not "" and 'instruction start' is 0 then, clear output.
  If problem is not "" and 'instruction start' is greater than 0 then, resize output to 'instruction start'.
  Return a text, problem.

(How many bytes the instruction takes, whatever its labels hold, or -1 when
 it cannot be encoded. The scratch buffer is the caller's, so measuring makes
 no buffer of its own.)
To 'the size of the instruction' with a map called record and a list called table and a list called 'row mnemonics' and a buffer called scratch.
  clear scratch.
  a text called problem is 'encode the record' with record and 0 and false and table and 'row mnemonics' and scratch.
  If problem is not "" then, Return a number, -1.
  Return a number, scratch's size.
